pmc:GhayaTilasmJamAlMal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Gathering wealth and trade"@en, "Reichtum und Handel sammeln"@de ;
    skos:definition "An image made after the ascendant and the tenth, their lords, the Moon and its lord and the lord of the ascendant have been made fortunate, the second and its lord fortunate and joined to the ascendant by trine or sextile with reception, the lot of fortune in the ascendant or the tenth joined to its lord and the lot of wealth regarded by its lord, the eleventh and its lord fortunate. Whoever holds the image becomes the richest of God's creatures, all trade comes easily to him, and he gains wealth in every movement."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: gathering wealth and trade, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 33" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmWilayaRafia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Obtaining a high office"@en, "Ein hohes Amt erlangen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Made after the ascendant, the tenth and its lord are made fortunate, the malefics kept from the ascendant and its lord, the lord of the eleventh a benefic regarding them, and the lord of the tenth joined to the lord of the ascendant in affection with full reception. Whoever holds it when he goes to the one from whom he seeks office is not passed over and his opinion not opposed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: obtaining a high office, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 33" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmMaylAlSultan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "A ruler inclining to a person and raising his rank"@en, "Ein Herrscher neigt sich einem Menschen zu und erhebt seinen Rang"@de ;
    skos:definition "An image made in the person's name, the ascendant made fortunate by a strong benefic not retrograde, cadent or burned, its lord strong and direct, the lord of the tenth joined to it by trine or sextile with reception, the lord of the ascendant in the commanding signs and the lord of the tenth in the obeying. Whoever keeps it meets no man of power who does not incline to him and raise his rank."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: a ruler inclining to a person and raising his rank, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 33-34" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmIstitafSayyid a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "A slave winning his master's favour"@en, "Ein Sklave gewinnt die Gunst seines Herrn"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two talismans, one in the hour of a superior planet with a superior ascendant, the Moon increasing in light and the Head with the ascendant or in an angle, the other in the hour of an inferior planet with the tenth of the first ascendant rising and the Tail in the ascendant or an angle; they are joined in an embrace and buried at the place of the one seeking favour, and the master turns to him wholly and grants all his needs."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin chapter has no talisman under a heading for this purpose that could be matched to this one, and the node cites the Arabic and Atallah only."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: a slave winning his master's favour, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 34" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmZawaj a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Marriage for one who wished it and was refused"@en, "Heirat für einen, der sie wünschte und abgewiesen wurde"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two figures, the first in the hour of Jupiter with Virgo rising and the Moon increasing in an angle, the second in the hour of Venus with Venus regarding Jupiter and Saturn and Mars fallen from the ascendant, the second's ascendant the seventh of the first and its significator joined to the first's from its triplicity; they are embraced and buried at the seeker's place."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin chapter has no talisman under a heading for this purpose that could be matched to this one, and the node cites the Arabic and Atallah only."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: marriage for one who wished it and was refused, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 34-35" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmTatilZawaj a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Preventing a man's marriage to a woman"@en, "Die Heirat eines Mannes mit einer Frau verhindern"@de ;
    skos:definition "One talisman with Leo rising in the hour of the Sun and another with Cancer rising in the hour of the Moon, the Moon increasing in light and swift; they are joined and buried in the hour of Venus, and he will never marry her."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin chapter has no talisman under a heading for this purpose that could be matched to this one, and the node cites the Arabic and Atallah only."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: preventing a man's marriage to a woman, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 35" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmIkhrajMasjun a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Bringing a prisoner out"@en, "Einen Gefangenen herausbringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Made in the hour of the Moon, increasing in light, free of the malefics and swift; buried in the tenth of the ascendant of the city with the face of the image turned towards the house of the imam."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: bringing a prisoner out, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 35" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmZafarBiAduw a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Prevailing over an enemy one wishes destroyed"@en, "Über einen Feind siegen, dessen Untergang man will"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two images, one with Leo rising in the hour of the Sun and the Moon cadent, the other with Cancer rising in the hour of Mars, cadent likewise; the first is made to take whatever vengeance one wishes on the second, and they are buried in the hour of Mars with Scorpio rising in its first face. Then one asks for his destruction in whatever way one wishes, and he is destroyed within the hour."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: prevailing over an enemy one wishes destroyed, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 35" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmRidaAlRaiyya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "The subjects of a rebellious town returning to their governor"@en, "Die Untertanen einer aufständischen Stadt kehren zu ihrem Statthalter zurück"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two talismans, the first in the hour of Jupiter with the Moon joined to the Sun in a fortunate shape, free of malefics, and the Head in or regarding the ascendant, the second with the fifth of the first rising in the hour of Venus, Venus with the Head or regarding it; both are buried in the hour of Saturn with a fixed sign rising, and all the subjects return to him and give him the utmost affection."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: the subjects of a rebellious town returning to their governor, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 36" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmImsakFiBaladih a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Keeping a man in his town"@en, "Einen Menschen in seiner Stadt halten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Made with the man's own ascendant rising and Saturn and the Head in an angle of it, buried upside down in the middle of the town with a fixed sign rising; he does not leave the town as long as the talisman stays buried."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin chapter has no talisman under a heading for this purpose that could be matched to this one, and the node cites the Arabic and Atallah only."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: keeping a man in his town, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 36" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmNafyMinYawmih a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Driving a man out of his town the same day"@en, "Einen Menschen noch am selben Tag aus seiner Stadt treiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "An image with a movable sign rising and its significator and the Moon withdrawn from the angles, buried in the burnt part of the way with its face turned towards the place to which one wishes him driven."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: driving a man out of his town the same day, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 36" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmIjtimaShakhsayn a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Two persons brought together in friendship that does not part"@en, "Zwei Menschen in einer Freundschaft vereint, die nicht zerbricht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two talismans, the first with the ascendant of the one sought, the ascendant and the tenth made fortunate and the malefics kept away, the lord of the eleventh a benefic joined to the lord of the ascendant by trine or sextile with reception; the second with the eleventh rising for a friend or the seventh for a spouse, its significator joined to the lord of the first; buried together at the seeker's place, and they agree and do not part."@en ;
    skos:note "The recipe carries the doctrine that grounds the whole chapter's use of aspects: trine joins signs of one element and is an aspect of friendship and affection, sextile joins fire to air and earth to water and is friendship without love, square joins opposed natures and is estrangement and hatred."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin chapter has no talisman under a heading for this purpose that could be matched to this one, and the node cites the Arabic and Atallah only."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: two persons brought together in friendship that does not part, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 37" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmFurqa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Separation and enmity"@en, "Trennung und Feindschaft"@de ;
    skos:definition "An image with Libra rising, the ascendant, the tenth and both their lords strongly afflicted by opposition or square without reception and stripped of the benefics; buried, and the two separate and never come together, for the great hatred that arises between them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: separation and enmity, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 38" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmIhlakAlKhadim a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "A ruler destroying one he employs"@en, "Ein Herrscher vernichtet einen, den er in Dienst hat"@de ;
    skos:definition "An image made by the same ratios as the last, with the lord of the ascendant turned away from the lord of the tenth and afflicted by it, and joined to the lord of the house of death and afflicted by it by opposition or square; buried with a fixed afflicted sign rising, and he kills his servant in any case and for any cause."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin chapter has no talisman under a heading for this purpose that could be matched to this one, and the node cites the Arabic and Atallah only."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: a ruler destroying one he employs, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 38" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmUlfaWaInqiyad a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Affection and submission"@en, "Zuneigung und Ergebenheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two talismans with Venus rising in the first face of Cancer and the Moon in the first face of Taurus, Venus in the ascendant and the Moon in the eleventh; they are set embracing and buried at the place of one of them, and this is lasting love and strong affection. The text calls the relation the ratio of exchange and refers to the thirty-third saying of Ptolemy's Fruit, promising to explain it in the fourth book."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin chapter has no talisman under a heading for this purpose that could be matched to this one, and the node cites the Arabic and Atallah only."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: affection and submission, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 38-39" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmAadadMutahabba a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "The talisman of the loving numbers"@en, "Der Talisman der befreundeten Zahlen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two talismans made with a fortunate ascendant while the Moon and Venus are in Taurus; on the first are written two hundred and twenty units, as thousands or as zeros, and on the second two hundred and eighty-four; they are set embracing and buried at the place of one of them, and lasting love and strong affection follow."@en ;
    skos:note "The two numbers are the smallest pair of amicable numbers, each the sum of the proper divisors of the other; the text names the talisman after the relation and does not state the arithmetic. The Latin keeps the numbers and writes the units as the cipher of algorism."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: the talisman of the loving numbers, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 39" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmSaydAlHut a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Catching fish in a river"@en, "Fische in einem Fluss fangen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The figure of a fish of the kind found in that river is made with Pisces rising and Jupiter in it in the hour of Venus, head first, then body, then tail, joined at the hour; it is set on a thin silver rod standing upright in a lead vessel filled with water and sealed so that nothing runs out, and the vessel thrown to the bottom of the river; the fish come to it from every side."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: catching fish in a river, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 39" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmNafyAlAqarib a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Driving scorpions away"@en, "Skorpione vertreiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "A scorpion of gold made with the Moon in the ascendant or an angle in Taurus, Aquarius or Leo, Leo being best because its nature is contrary to the scorpion's, the Sun in Leo in its hour and Saturn retrograde; it is made in reversed order, tail, legs, arms and head last, the left and right limbs set in each other's places, a thorn fixed in its back so that it stings its own head; buried in a pierced mineral stone and the stone in the noblest place of the city, and the scorpions flee the talisman for forty-five miles."@en ;
    skos:note "The distance is Ritter's text and the Latin's, forty-five miles in every direction. Atallah has forty-five days over one mile, which is a reading of Ritter's apparatus; under that reading the recipe would carry a term, and under the text it carries none."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: driving scorpions away, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 39-40" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmLadghAlAqrab a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Curing a scorpion sting"@en, "Einen Skorpionstich heilen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A scorpion is engraved on a bezoar stone in the hour of the Moon with the Moon in the beginning of the second face of Scorpio and Leo, Taurus or Aquarius rising; the stone is set in a gold ring and stamped in chewed frankincense at the same configuration, and the stung man is given the impression to drink and is cured of his pain."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: curing a scorpion sting, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 40" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmIltilafAlRijalWaAlNisa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Men and women drawn to one another"@en, "Männer und Frauen einander zugeneigt"@de ;
    skos:definition "A talisman in the form of a girl, of a cold metal, with Virgo rising and Mercury in it ascending to the top of its apogee and ruling, begun in the hour of Mercury and finished, and another in the form of a man with Mercury returned to Virgo or in Gemini; one must beware that the ascendants differ, Mercury in Virgo with Gemini rising or the reverse; the two are set embracing, each with its hands on the other's back, all in the hour of Mercury, and buried in the busiest road of the city; men and women then come to agree. The same is done for one person with another, buried at a place both frequent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: men and women drawn to one another, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 40-41" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmSaydAlHitanKhwarizmi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Catching fish, after al-Khwārizmī"@en, "Fische fangen, nach al-Khwārizmī"@de ;
    skos:definition "The text says it found this in a letter of Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Khwārizmī, who claimed he had tested it: a fish is cast with the first face of Pisces rising, the Moon and Mercury in it, in the hour of the Moon, and held at the time of fishing; it gives strong help in the work."@en ;
    skos:note "One of two recipes of Book I that carry a claim of trial, and the trial is ascribed, not reported: the compiler read that al-Khwārizmī said he tried it. The Latin gives the source as Alhanemi and strengthens the claim, saying he tried it and found it true."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: catching fish, after al-Khwārizmī, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 41" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmAlTabib a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Drawing those who seek advice to a physician"@en, "Ratsuchende zu einem Arzt ziehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A talisman physicians should like to make: on a tin plate a seated man is engraved with the instruments of medicine before him and people standing with flasks of water asking his counsel, the engraving covering the plate, with one of the two houses of Venus rising and Mars in it and the Head at the midheaven; the plate is nailed up at the place one keeps, and one will see wonders."@en ;
    skos:note "Atallah titles it bringing the physician to the place of treatment; the picture of people standing with flasks before the seated physician, and the text's own words that physicians should wish to make it, show the clients coming to him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: drawing those who seek advice to a physician, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 41" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmGhirasat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Plantings and tillage that thrive"@en, "Pflanzungen und Feldbau, die gedeihen"@de ;
    skos:definition "On a silver plate a man sitting among crops and plantings is pictured with Taurus rising and the Moon in the ascendant, separating from the Sun and joined to Saturn; buried where one wishes, and whatever is sown there flourishes, bears quickly, and is protected from beasts, cold, birds and every harm."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: plantings and tillage that thrive, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 42" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmAlTajir a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "A merchant prospering"@en, "Ein Kaufmann, dem es gut geht"@de ;
    skos:definition "A man with a balance in his hand is pictured on a plate of brass, ṣufr, with one of the two houses of Mercury rising and the Moon in it; whoever holds it sees great good in his trade."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin chapter has no talisman under a heading for this purpose that could be matched to this one, and the node cites the Arabic and Atallah only."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: a merchant prospering, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 42" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmAlHasa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Relief from the pain of the stone"@en, "Linderung vom Schmerz des Steins"@de ;
    skos:definition "On a gold plate a lion is engraved with stones before its paws as if playing with them, in the hour of the Sun, the beginning of the middle face of Leo rising and the Sun in that degree; when the sufferer from the stone holds it, the pain of the stone leaves him. The text adds: and this is tried."@en ;
    skos:note "The second recipe of Book I that claims a trial, and the claim is the compiler's, in two words, with no case, number or witness. The Latin has it as very often proved."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: relief from the pain of the stone, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 42" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmSawdawiyya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Driving off melancholic illnesses and securing health"@en, "Melancholische Krankheiten vertreiben und die Gesundheit festigen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Made in the hour of Venus with the Moon in an angle joined to Venus, the lord of the sixth in trine or opposition of a benefic, the lord of the eighth in square of Mercury, Mercury not retrograde, burned or looking at a malefic, in the last hour of Sunday with the lord of the hour the tenth from the ascendant, of pure silver; it drives off the melancholic illnesses, establishes the health of whom one wishes until it is sound and firm, and wards off attacks."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: driving off melancholic illnesses and securing health, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 42-43" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTilasmRasdAlYunaniyyin a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "The Greek sages' way of asking a planet for what they sought"@en, "Die Art der griechischen Weisen, einen Planeten um das Gewünschte zu bitten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Greek sages watched a planet when it was with its node and set it at the degree of the midheaven, fumigated with its incense, called with its prayer and offered its sacrifice for the thing they wanted, and their request succeeded; they did the same, the text says, when it was with the Tail of its node, and it was then unhindered, above all when the planet had a strong testimony in the nativity of the one asking, which makes its effect plainer and its acceptance surer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the talisman: the Greek sages' way of asking a planet for what they sought, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 47" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

#
# The spoken formulas of I.5
#
# The chapter gives six formulas to be said over the talisman, with the rule
# that the words must agree with the intent and be spoken with the whole of
# the maker's strength. Two of them, for love and for enmity, belong to recipes
# above; four name purposes no recipe of the chapter names, and are entered as
# recipes of their own.
#

pmc:GhayaKalamAqdAlShahwa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Binding a man's desire"@en, "Das Begehren eines Mannes binden"@de ;
    skos:definition "For a talisman binding desire one says: I have bound the spirituality of the desire of so-and-so towards such-and-such a woman, or towards all women, and seized it by the power of these spiritual spirits as mountains are bound."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin turns this formula into its opposite purpose: its words bind the spirit of a man's will to one woman or to all women, a binding of attachment, where the Arabic binds his desire away from them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the spoken formulas, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 45" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaKalamHallAlShahwa a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Releasing a bound desire"@en, "Ein gebundenes Begehren lösen"@de ;
    skos:definition "For releasing one says: I have loosed and undone the bonds of the spirituality of the bound desire of so-and-so towards such-and-such a woman, or all women, by the power of these spirits, as fire loosens wax, as the shining Sun releases the darkness of the world, and as snow melts in the Sun."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the spoken formulas, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 45" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaKalamQatAlAlsina a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Cutting off people's tongues from oneself or another"@en, "Die Zungen der Leute von sich oder einem anderen abschneiden"@de ;
    skos:definition "For a talisman to cut off people's tongues from oneself or another one says: I have covered so-and-so with the cover of the shining light, cut off people's tongues from him, and let down over their eyes a spiritual veil that repels their wicked looks and cuts off the ways of their hurtful designs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the spoken formulas, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 45" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaKalamHatkAlSitr a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Exposing a person"@en, "Einen Menschen bloßstellen"@de ;
    skos:definition "For exposing a man one says: I have torn the veil of so-and-so by the power of these spirits as the Sun's rays tear thick cloud, disgraced him and made him a target for the spirituality of the tongues as the arrows that archers loose upon one mark."@en ;
    skos:note "The Latin gives the purpose as making a man ill spoken of and hated by people."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the spoken formulas, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 45" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

#
# The prerequisites
#

pmc:GhayaTamamAlAmal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "The talismanic work completed and the sought effect present"@en, "Das talismanische Werk vollendet und die gesuchte Wirkung da"@de ;
    skos:definition "What the conditions of I.4 and I.5 are conditions of: the joining of the maker's soul and will to the powers of the heavens by which the thing sought comes to be; in the book's own terms, receptivity, then reception, then the union of matter and form, then the appearance of the act intended."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.4, on preparedness, and I.5, on the conditions, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 28, 43" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.4 and I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaJamAlHimma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "The maker's gathered resolve and corrected intention"@en, "Die gesammelte Entschlossenheit und berichtigte Absicht des Herstellers"@de ;
    skos:definition "The maker gathers his whole resolve into the work and makes his intention sound, so that the powers of his soul are bound to the powers of the heavens; the text supports it from Plato's Book of Aphorisms, that speech agreeing with the intention of the speaker moves the intention of the hearer, and finds the same ground in the joining of intention to prayer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, on the conditions, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 43" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaHimmaBeforeTamam a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Nothing of the work is completed without the maker's gathered resolve"@en, "Ohne die gesammelte Entschlossenheit des Herstellers wird nichts vom Werk vollendet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Among the conditions of talismans is one without which nothing of them is completed: that the maker gather his resolve into his work and make his intention sound, so that the powers of his soul are bound to the powers of the heavens."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:GhayaTamamAlAmal ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:GhayaJamAlHimma ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Nothing of the talismanic work is completed; the text gives this as the mark of the condition, among the others it lists, that none of it is completed without it."@en ;
    skos:note "The only condition of the first book that the text marks as necessary rather than as good practice, and the one that cannot be checked from outside: a talisman that failed can always be said to have been made without it. The Latin, at I.4, adds to the parallel requirement of certainty a clause the Arabic does not have — that the operators must not work to test or prove whether these things are true, but believe them true in their will — which turns a condition of the work into a rule against the trial of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, on the conditions of talismans, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 43" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaYaqin a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "The maker's certainty, without doubt, of what he does"@en, "Die zweifelsfreie Gewissheit des Herstellers in dem, was er tut"@de ;
    skos:definition "The maker of talismans must know the equations and astral ratios and be certain of the soundness of what he does, with no doubt or misgiving entering his work. The text calls this the maker's preparedness, istiʿdād, a disposing of himself to receive the acts sought, and says it exists only in the human kind."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.4, opening and on preparedness, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 19, 28" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.4, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaYaqinBeforeTamam a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "The maker's certainty comes before the effect"@en, "Die Gewissheit des Herstellers geht der Wirkung voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The maker must be certain and without doubt so that the act of the rational soul is strengthened and the will is joined to its world from the world-soul, and the thing sought comes to be."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:GhayaTamamAlAmal ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:GhayaYaqin ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    skos:note "Set as prescribed order: the text gives the purpose of the certainty and not what follows from its lack. The Latin adds the clause against working to test or prove, noted at pmc:GhayaHimmaBeforeTamam."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.4, opening, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 19" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.4, opening, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaIstidadAlMadda a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "The receptivity of the matter a talisman is made of"@en, "Die Empfänglichkeit des Stoffes, aus dem ein Talisman gemacht wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "Preparedness in things other than man is a natural passivity, as wax is disposed to take a form and the one thrown is disposed to be thrown by the weakness of his limbs; this kind is needed in the matters from which talismans are made, since not every matter receives every act. All the books, the text says, agree on this root."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.4, on preparedness, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 28" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.4, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaIstidadBeforeTamam a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "A receptive matter comes before the effect"@en, "Ein empfänglicher Stoff geht der Wirkung voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "When there is preparedness there is reception, when there is reception there is union and the act sought appears; the union is the impression by which matter and form become one, as a man's form in water or a mirror."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:GhayaTamamAlAmal ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:GhayaIstidadAlMadda ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    skos:note "A chain of three steps stated as following one from another, preparedness, reception, union, and stated positively only: the text does not say what a talisman of an unreceptive matter does, and the condition is therefore entered as an order and not as a necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.4, on preparedness, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 28" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.4, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaQamarMuwafiq a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "The Moon at the degree fitting the work"@en, "Der Mond in dem Grad, der dem Werk entspricht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Do no work of the works until the Moon is in the degree fitting that work, for the Moon has manifest acts in the world nearest ours; by day the Moon in the ascendant with a diurnal sign rising, by night a nocturnal one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.4, opening and close, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 19, 28" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.4, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaQamarBeforeAmal a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "No work before the Moon stands at the fitting degree"@en, "Kein Werk, bevor der Mond im passenden Grad steht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The chapter on the mansions opens with the rule that no work is to be done until the Moon is in the degree fitting it, and gives the twenty-eight mansions as the table by which the degree is chosen."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:GhayaTamamAlAmal ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:GhayaQamarMuwafiq ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    skos:note "An instruction without a stated consequence at this place; the consequences of a wrong configuration are stated separately, for the ascendant, at pmc:GhayaTaliMuwajj, and for one mansion and one work at pmc:GhayaManzilaSadAlSuud."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.4, opening, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 19" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.4, opening, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaIstitar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Working hidden from people and from the sunrise, with only trusted company"@en, "Verborgen vor den Menschen und dem Sonnenaufgang arbeiten, nur in vertrauter Gesellschaft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Among the conditions of the work: that the maker conceal himself from men and their sight and from the rising of the Sun and its light, and that none look on him but one of sound resolve and trusted companionship, who neither makes light of nor scorns any of the works, wondrous and overpowering in this world, that proceed from the spiritualities of the heavens."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, on the conditions, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 43-44" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaIstitarBeforeAmal a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Concealment and trusted company come before the work"@en, "Verborgenheit und vertraute Gesellschaft gehen dem Werk voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The conditions of the work include concealment from men and from the Sun's light and the exclusion of any onlooker who would make light of or scorn its effects."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:GhayaTamamAlAmal ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:GhayaIstitar ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    skos:note "Stated as a condition and without a consequence. Its exclusion of the scornful onlooker is the shape the holding records elsewhere as the unsympathetic witness named as a cause of failure, but this text names no failure: it excludes the witness before the work and does not explain a failure after it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, on the conditions of talismans, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 43-44" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

#
# The conditions of failure
#

pmc:GhayaTilasmMayyit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "A talisman made outside its ratios is like a dead body"@en, "Ein außerhalb seiner Verhältnisse gefertigter Talisman ist wie ein toter Leib"@de ;
    skos:definition "Citing Thābit ibn Qurra's treatise on talismans, that the most exalted knowledge of the stars is the knowledge of talismans and that there is no life in a body without a spirit, the text explains: talismans made outside the ratios intended for them do not receive the spreading of the planets' spiritualities and are like dead bodies with no spirit in them; made on the true astral ratios fitting the purpose, they receive them and are like living bodies that do strange things."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure and also an account of every failure: a talisman that did nothing was made outside its ratios. The two cannot be told apart from the text, because nothing but the result is offered as the sign of a wrong ratio."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, Thābit ibn Qurra's saying, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 44" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaTaliMuwajj a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "A crooked rising or a malefic aspect spoils the work"@en, "Ein krumm aufsteigender Aszendent oder ein übler Aspekt verdirbt das Werk"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the ascendant is of the signs of straight rising, the work goes easily and is carried through; if of crooked rising, it is made hard. Its soundness or ruin, however, lies with the aspects: a straight-rising ascendant with a malefic in it or regarding it spoils the work and makes it hard, a crooked one regarded by a benefic eases it; and diurnal signs rising by night or nocturnal by day are strengthened by the benefics and ruined by the malefics."@en ;
    skos:note "Stated as the outcome of a configuration the maker chooses, and so checkable in principle against a record of works and their ascendants; the book keeps no such record."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.4, on the ascendant, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 28-29" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.4, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

#
# The cautions
#

pmc:GhayaIstinzalYaqtul a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Some divine names bring the spirits down so fast that they may kill the one who draws them"@en, "Manche göttliche Namen bringen die Geister so schnell herab, dass sie den töten können, der sie herabzieht"@de ;
    skos:definition "In a treatise the text ascribes to Aristotle: among the divine names there are some by which, when the spiritualities are drawn down, they fall headlong and plunge downwards, and perhaps they kill the one drawing them down if he does not know the nature of the spirituality of the planet he draws down."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:GhayaIstinzalAlRuhaniyya ;
    skos:note "The only warning of the first book that names harm to the operator, and its condition is knowledge: the danger is to the one who does not know the nature of the spirit. The text sets beside it the Sufis' Greatest Name, by which things are changed from what they are, and says that the common run of the Sufis believe otherwise, referring to a treatise of its own on the question; and it continues the Aristotle passage with the magical incantations, which do not pass beyond the circle of the earth unless the hope placed in them is bound to the greatest God."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, the sayings of Aristotle, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 44" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, Aristoteles, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaKusufAlQamar a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Avoid the eclipsed or burned Moon for works of good"@en, "Den verfinsterten oder verbrannten Mond bei guten Werken meiden"@de ;
    skos:definition "For talismans of good one is to avoid as far as one can the Moon in eclipse and wait until it is free, the Moon within twelve degrees of the Sun before or after, Mars or Saturn within twelve degrees, the Moon descending in southern latitude, slow in course, in the burnt way from the eighteenth degree of Libra to the third of Scorpio, at the ends of the signs that are the malefics' terms, or cadent from the midheaven into the ninth; burning by the Sun is the worst misfortune. When a necessary work cannot wait, the Moon is mended by setting Jupiter and Venus in the ascendant or the midheaven."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:GhayaSinaatAlTilasm ;
    skos:note "A warning with its own exception, and the exception is a correction inside the system rather than a waiver of it: urgency does not suspend the configuration, it calls for a second configuration to offset the first."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.4, on the impediments of the Moon, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 29-30" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.4, close, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaKalamKharijAnAlQasd a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Speak no word outside the intent while working"@en, "Während der Arbeit kein Wort außerhalb der Absicht sprechen"@de ;
    skos:definition "While at the work one is not to speak words outside the intended purpose, but words that strengthen the work and carry it through; the formulas of the chapter are given as examples so that the intent is understood, and the maker may add like words with true intention and strong resolve."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:GhayaSinaatAlTilasm ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.5, after the spoken formulas, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, p. 45" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.5, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

#
# The one yielding of I.6
#

pmc:GhayaMushahada a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "True sight of the ranks of the human form"@en, "Wahre Schau der Rangstufen der menschlichen Gestalt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Seeing and witnessing in truth what the sixth chapter teaches: that the human body is the shell of the partial man, the partial man the shell of the universal man, and so upwards through the universal soul and intellect to the light from which intellect was made, each higher the matter of the lower; the text calls this the foundation of the science of talismans and plain magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.6, close, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix (Ghayat al-Hakim), The Goal of the Wise, trans. Hashem Atallah, vol. I, pp. 56-57" ;
    dcterms:source "Picatrix Latinus I.6, ed. D. Pingree (London: Warburg Institute, 1986)" .

pmc:GhayaFadilaYieldsMushahada a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:GhayatAlHakim ;
    rdfs:label "Virtue and purity of mind and body bring the true sight of it"@en, "Tugend und Reinheit an Sinn und Leib bringen die wahre Schau davon"@de ;
    skos:definition "Whoever wishes to know this in truth, let him be virtuous, clean of mind and body from defilements, for he will see it and witness it with true witnessing."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:GhayaFadilaWaNaqa ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:GhayaMushahada ;
    skos:note "The only claim of the first book that a practice of the person, and not a talisman, brings about a capacity; it stands in the chapter of the book that is not about talismans at all and calls itself their foundation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:IslamicLetterMagic ;
    dcterms:source "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm I.6, close, ed. H. Ritter (1933)" ;
